0678510214826_5066401859479633085_n.png?_nc_cat=104&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=6e7a0a&_nc_ohc=Pkl0mjmoNroQ7kNvwG_N0VR&_nc_oc=AdpCLpRyQhIJKzQPledscZeTqTfIR12HcoQ6gDYUOSJm46AMr5s6MJeUKddZY0iPd9I&_nc_zt=3&_nc_ht=scontent-atl3-1.oculuscdn.com&_nc_ss=7f30f&oh=00_AQQbhBCMebmGS4i6R0SWEiU3O-SaXJ3JvwJKE-8kIk9yzQ&oe=6B0B1826"}, {"file_name": "vlcsnap-2026-09-04-15h51m42s830.png", "id": "2180763875803507", "uri": "https://scontent-atl3-1.oculuscdn.com/v/t64.5771-25/491824671_2180763879136840_6253815126760550249_n.png?_nc_cat=108&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=6e7a0a&_nc_ohc=4vlrR6fknh4Q7kNvwEuk4to&_nc_oc=AdqLxOx-O6LzKuXVGXCyp7XINexwQpdyUuIR7jflTZhisXxOcBRvwa23KlmIiQjmnIo&_nc_zt=3&_nc_ht=scontent-atl3-1.oculuscdn.com&_nc_ss=7f30f&oh=00_AQQpm1yPHNnU2ho_5HG31bz-fpN3xvf_ClO4cRat8kkasw&oe=6B0B0EC3"}, {"file_name": "vlcsnap-2026-09-04-15h52m01s472.png", "id": "1547585189878266", "uri": "https://scontent-atl3-1.oculuscdn.com/v/t64.5771-25/491823783_1547585196544932_5404206270363821866_n.png?_nc_cat=109&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=6e7a0a&_nc_ohc=Af2-uV37MLAQ7kNvwFNyfdS&_nc_oc=Adpp4RUzixA3zC2IHHzo2hn3UwAEeIWxbGoaTx6Znf-Eb-iHcK1Xz4VJdmL_dYKrDlI&_nc_zt=3&_nc_ht=scontent-atl3-1.oculuscdn.com&_nc_ss=7f30f&oh=00_AQTzGtd1nRrFXC9wAhWer6sP9jt3X4rUC8gkO_LdQ52DCQ&oe=6B0B2D8D"}], "video_trailer": null, "video_trailer_thumbnail": null, "display_name": "Nevrolens", "keywords": ["nevrolens", "neuroanatomy", "brain", "augmentedreality"], "long_description": "# About the app\nNevrolens is an augmented reality application designed to support interactive learning in comparative neuroanatomy. The app features high-resolution 3D brain models of five mammalian species: two rodents (mouse and rat), two non-human primates (marmoset and macaque), and human.\nOriginally developed for medical and neuroscience students, the app allows to place brain models into physical space and interact with them. This immersive format supports formal instruction and self-directed learning.\n\n# This is how you use the app\n\nThe app allows you to explore the brain models and to test the acquired knowledge. The main features include: \n* Brain model morphing that allows cross-species neuroanatomy comparison\n* Brain dissection can be used to view custom or fixed planes (horizontal, coronal and sagittal).\n* Manipulation of the brain models (move, rotate and scale)\n* Model interaction, pointing at different brain parts, moving them to reveal the underlying structures\n* Textual descriptions of the different brain parts\n\n# Conditions\nThe app requests access to the camera of the user\u2019s device to enable the augmented reality experience.\n\nThe app does not access the user's personal data stored on the device. The app does not access the user's location data. The app does not collect data about the user\u2019s device.\n\nThe app is being continuously developed. Functions and content can be altered without a warning. The developer does not take any responsibility for use, errors or loss of user data.", "short_description": "Nevrolens is an augmented reality application designed to support interactive learning in comparative neuroanatomy. The app features high-resolution 3D brain models of five mammalian species: two rodents (mouse and rat), two non-human primates (marmoset and macaque), and human.\nOriginally developed for medical and neuroscience students, the app allows to place brain models into physical space and interact with them. This immersive format supports formal instruction and self-directed learning."}]}, "developer_name": null, "developer_privacy_policy_url": "https://www.ntnu.edu/imtel/nevrolens#conditions-and-privacy", "developer_terms_of_service_url": null, "publisher_name": "Norwegian University of Science and Technology", "support_website_url": null, "website_url": "https://www.ntnu.edu/imtel/nevrolens", "category": null, "comfort_rating": "COMFORTABLE_FOR_MOST", "comfort_tags": [], "context_category": {"id": "362692211442873"